VERY important find at section 5 (lines 388–411):

```matlab
%%          %%% 5. DETERMINE POLARITY OF SPECTRUM (EG FOR MOIST WATER SUPP) %%%
            % Automate determination whether the Cr peak has positive polarity.
            ...
            if ~isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'polResidCr')
                if isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'PreservePolarity') && ~MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.PreservePolarity
                    raw_Cr     = op_freqrange(raw,2.8,3.2);
                    ...
                    polResidCr = abs(max(real(raw_Cr.specs))) - abs(min(real(raw_Cr.specs)));
                    polResidCr = squeeze(polResidCr);
                    polResidCr(polResidCr<0) = -1;
                    polResidCr(polResidCr>0) = 1;
                else
                    polResidCr = 1;
                end
            else
                polResidCr = MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.polResidCr;
            end
            raw = op_ampScale(raw,polResidCr);
            MRSCont.raw{metab_ll,kk} = op_ampScale(MRSCont.raw{metab_ll,kk},polResidCr);
```

So there IS a "PreservePolarity" option: `MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.PreservePolarity`. When set AND false → the process stage estimates the polarity of the Cr peak per subspectrum and flips sign so that Cr is positive (polarity-consistent). When NOT set (the default!) → `polResidCr = 1`, i.e., no polarity correction.

Hmm wait — read carefully:

```matlab
if isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'PreservePolarity') && ~MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.PreservePolarity
    % compute polResidCr from data
else
    polResidCr = 1;
end
```

So the polarity determination only happens when `PreservePolarity` field exists AND is false. When `PreservePolarity` is true → polResidCr=1 (preserve). When it doesn't exist → polResidCr=1 (preserve)!

Now, what is the UPSTREAM Osprey code here? From the actual Osprey repository (OspreyProcess.m, section 5):

```matlab
%%          %%% 5. DETERMINE POLARITY OF SPECTRUM (EG FOR MOIST WATER SUPP) %%%
            % Automate determination whether the Cr peak has positive polarity.
            % For water suppression methods like MOIST, the residual water may
            % actually have negative polarity, but end up positive in the data, so
            % that the spectrum needs to be flipped.
            if ~isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'polResidCr')
                if ~isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'PreservePolarity') || (isfield(MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment, 'PreservePolarity') && ~MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.PreservePolarity)
                    raw_Cr     = op_freqrange(raw,2.8,3.2);
                    % Determine the polarity of the respective peak: if the absolute of the
                    % maximum minus the absolute of the minimum is positive, the polarity
                    % of the respective peak is positive; if the absolute of the maximum
                    % minus the absolute of the minimum is negative, the polarity is negative.
                    polResidCr = abs(max(real(raw_Cr.specs))) - abs(min(real(raw_Cr.specs)));
                    polResidCr = squeeze(polResidCr);
                    polResidCr(polResidCr<0) = -1;
                    polResidCr(polResidCr>0) = 1;
                else
                    polResidCr = 1;
                end
            else
                polResidCr = MRSCont.opts.SubSpecAlignment.polResidCr;
            end
```

YES — this is it! I'm now quite confident: in upstream Osprey, the condition is "if PreservePolarity NOT set, OR (set AND false)" → i.e., polarity correction happens BY DEFAULT, and is skipped only when the user explicitly sets `PreservePolarity = true`.

In the snapshot it has been inverted to: "if PreservePolarity set AND false" → correction; otherwise (default) → no correction. So the corrected workflow default behavior (polarity-consistent Cr across subspectra) is disabled, and the loading-vs-processing semantics become inconsistent: the loader... hmm wait, but which of the two is the injected bug — the loader's dead phase-cycle-removal, or this inverted condition? The task hint says "may come from inconsistent semantics between data loading and later processing, NOT ONLY from the final alignment routine" — i.e., the fix may involve BOTH a load-stage piece and a process-stage piece. 

Let me now check `OspreySettings.m` to see whether `SubSpecAlignment.PreservePolarity` is set by default there.

